{:edb/db-before-t 257 :edb/db-after-t 258 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533570 50 #inst "2026-10-11T09:52:10.241Z" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186151957 1071 "Find the greatest common divisor of $1\\,036$ and $555$." 13194139533570 17592186045417 false] [17592186151957 1071 "Find the greatest common divisor of $1\\,036$ and $555.$" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186151957 1074 "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1036 &= \\begin{vmatrix}555(1) & +\\end{vmatrix}481 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 555 &= \\end{vmatrix}481(1)\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 74 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 481|= & 74(6)\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}37 \\\\ ↙\\end{vmatrix}↙ \\\\\n74 &= \\begin{vmatrix}37(2) & +\\end{vmatrix}0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{red}37}$. Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $1\\,036$ and $555$ is $37$. Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(1\\,036, 555) = 37\n$$" 13194139533570 17592186045417 false] [17592186151957 1074 "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}1036 & = & 555(1) & + & 481 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 555 & = & 481(1) & + & 74 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 481 & = & 74(6) & + & 37 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 74 & = & 37(2) & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{red}37}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $1\\,036$ and $555$ is $37.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(1\\,036, 555) = 37.\n$$" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186151993 1071 "Find the greatest common divisor of $713$ and $322$." 13194139533570 17592186045417 false] [17592186151993 1071 "Find the greatest common divisor of $713$ and $322.$" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186151993 1074 "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n713 &= \\begin{vmatrix}322(2) & +\\end{vmatrix}69 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 322 &= \\end{vmatrix}69(4)\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 46 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 69|= & 46(1)\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}23 \\\\ ↙\\end{vmatrix}↙ \\\\\n46 &= \\begin{vmatrix}23(2) & +\\end{vmatrix}0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{red}23}$. Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $713$ and $322$ is $23$. Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(713, 322) = 23\n$$" 13194139533570 17592186045417 false] [17592186151993 1074 "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}713 & = & 322(2) & + & 69 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 322 & = & 69(4) & + & 46 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 69 & = & 46(1) & + & 23 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 46 & = & 23(2) & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{red}23}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $713$ and $322$ is $23.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(713, 322) = 23.\n$$" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186152033 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 7$\n\n- $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 1$\n\n- $651$ and $574$ are coprime" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186152033 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 7$\n2. $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 1$\n3. $651$ and $574$ are coprime" 13194139533570 17592186045417 false] [17592186152033 1074 "Recall that two integers are coprime if their greatest common divisor is $1$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Indeed, let's use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n$651|=\\begin{vmatrix}574 \\cdot 1 & +\\end{vmatrix}77; ↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 574|=\\end{vmatrix}77 \\cdot 7\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 35 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 77|= & 35 \\cdot 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ ↙\\end{vmatrix}↙; 35|=\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\cdot 5 & +\\end{vmatrix}0$\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{blue}7}$. So, the greatest common divisor of $651$ and $574$ is $7$:\n$\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 7$.\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) > 1$, the numbers are not coprime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533570 17592186045417 false] [17592186152033 1074 "Recall that two integers are coprime if their greatest common divisor is $1.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Indeed, let's use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}651 & = & 574\\cdot 1 & + & 77 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 574 & = & 77\\cdot 7 & + & 35 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 77 & = & 35\\cdot 2 & + & 7 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 35 & = & 7\\cdot 5 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{blue}7}.$ So, the greatest common divisor of $651$ and $574$ is $7\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 7.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) > 1,$ the numbers are not coprime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186152166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409484 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186152191 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409481 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186152223 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409482 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186152223 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409483 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186152343 1050 600.0 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186152764 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151091 13194139533570 17592186045417 false] [17592186152764 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409476 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186152764 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186152764 1071 "Find the greatest common divisor of $296$ and $185$." 13194139533570 17592186045417 false] [17592186152764 1071 "Find the greatest common divisor of $296$ and $185.$" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186152764 1074 "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}296 & = & 185(1) & + & 111 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 185 & = & 111(1) & + & 74 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 111 & = & 74(1) & + & 37 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 74 & = & 37(2) & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $37.$ Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $296$ and $185$ is $37.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(296,185)=37.\n$$" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186152805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151337 13194139533570 17592186045417 false] [17592186152805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409478 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186152805 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186152805 1071 "Find the greatest common divisor of $1248$ and $816$." 13194139533570 17592186045417 false] [17592186152805 1071 "Find the greatest common divisor of $1\\,248$ and $816.$" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186152805 1074 "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}1\\,248 & = & 816(1) & + & 432 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 816 & = & 432(1) & + & 384 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 432 & = & 384(1) & + & 48 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 384 & = & 48(8) & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $48.$ Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $1\\,248$ and $816$ is $48.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(1\\,248,816)=48.\n$$" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186153356 1064 "The **greatest common divisor** (or $\\textrm{gcd}$) of two integers is the largest positive integer that divides both of the integers. For example,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(48,18) = 6\n$$\n\nbecause $6$ is the largest integer that divides both $48$ and $18$.\n\nWe often wish to compute the greatest common divisor of two large numbers. Doing this by inspection is hard. Another method is to use prime factorization, but this is also difficult and inefficient for large numbers.\n\nAnother method, known as the **Euclidian algorithm** is very efficient, despite being over $2\\,000$ years old.\n\nLet's demonstrate how to compute $\\textrm{gcd}(48,18)$ using the Euclidian algorithm. The first step is to apply the division algorithm and write down\n\n$$\n48 = 18q + r\n$$\n\nWhen we do, we get\n\n$$\n48 = {\\color{blue}{18}}(2) + {\\color{red}12}\n$$\n\nWe ignore the quotient $2$ for now and focus on the ${\\color{blue}{18}}$ (the smaller of our two numbers) and the remainder ${\\color{red}12}$. We apply the division algorithm again to these two numbers, and this gives\n\n$$\n18 = {\\color{blue}{12}}(1) + {\\color{red}6}\n$$\n\nApplying the division algorithm to the number ${\\color{blue}{12}}$ and the remainder ${\\color{red}6}$ gives\n\n$$\n12 = {\\color{blue}{6}}(2) + {\\color{red}0}\n$$\n\nWe have now reached a remainder of ${\\color{red}0}$, which means that we are done.\n\nIn practice, we normally write the steps underneath one another, like so.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n48 &= \\begin{vmatrix}18(2) & +\\end{vmatrix}12 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 18 &= \\end{vmatrix}12(1)\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 6 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 12|= & 6(2)\\end{vmatrix}+ \\mid 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{red}6}$. The Euclidean algorithm states that this is the greatest common divisor that we seek. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(48,18) = {\\color{red}6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we note that two integers $a$ and $b$ are **coprime** if\n\n$$\n\\gcd(a,b) = 1\n$$" 13194139533570 17592186045417 false] [17592186153356 1064 "The **greatest common divisor** (or $\\textrm{gcd}$) of two integers is the largest positive integer that divides both of the integers. For example,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(48,18) = 6\n$$\n\nbecause $6$ is the largest integer that divides both $48$ and $18.$\n\nWe often wish to compute the greatest common divisor of two large numbers. Doing this by inspection is hard. Another method is to use prime factorization, but this is also difficult and inefficient for large numbers. \n\nAnother method, known as the **Euclidian algorithm** is very efficient, despite being over $2\\,000$ years old. \n\nLet's demonstrate how to compute $\\textrm{gcd}(48,18)$ using the Euclidian algorithm. The first step is to apply the division algorithm and write down $48 = 18q + r.$ When we do, we get\n\n$$\n48 = {\\color{blue}{18}}(2) + {\\color{red}12}.\n$$\n\nWe ignore the quotient $2$ for now and focus on the ${\\color{blue}{18}}$ (the smaller of our two numbers) and the remainder ${\\color{red}12}.$ We apply the division algorithm again to these two numbers, and this gives\n\n$$\n18 = {\\color{blue}{12}}(1) + {\\color{red}6}.\n$$\n\nApplying the division algorithm to the number ${\\color{blue}{12}}$ and the remainder ${\\color{red}6}$ gives\n\n$$\n12 = {\\color{blue}{6}}(2) + {\\color{red}0}.\n$$\n\nWe have now reached a remainder of ${\\color{red}0},$ which means that we are done.\n\nIn practice, we normally write the steps underneath one another, like so.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}48 & = & 18(2) & + & 12 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 18 & = & 12(1) & + & 6 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 12 & = & 6(2) & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{red}6}.$ The Euclidean algorithm states that this is the greatest common divisor that we seek. Therefore, we conclude that $\\textrm{gcd}(48,18) = {\\color{red}6}.$\n\nFinally, we note that two integers $a$ and $b$ are **coprime** if $\\gcd(a,b) = 1.$" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186153380 1064 "We now return to the question of why the Euclidean algorithm works. To help us to understand, let's consider once more how to compute $\\textrm{gcd}(48,18)$. Using the algorithm, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n48 &= \\begin{vmatrix}18(2) & +\\end{vmatrix}12 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 18 &= \\end{vmatrix}12(1)\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 6 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 12|= & 6(2)\\end{vmatrix}+ \\mid 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCarrying out the process gives the following pairs of integers:\n\n$$\n48,18\\quad \\longrightarrow\\quad 18,12 \\quad \\longrightarrow \\quad 12,6\n$$\n\nWe claim that $\\textrm{gcd}(48,18)$ must be the same as $\\textrm{gcd}(18,12)$. To see why, notice that we used the division algorithm to write\n\n$$\n48 = 18(2) + 12\n$$\n\nIf $48$ and $18$ are divisible by some integer $n$, then $n$ must also divide $18(2)$. This means that $n$ must divide $48-18(2)$, i.e., $n$ must divide $12$. So any number $n$ that divides both $48$ and $18$ must also divide both $18$ and $12$.\n\nRepeating the same argument, we are able to conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(18,12) = \\textrm{gcd}(12,6)\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(12,6) = 6\n$$\n\nwe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(48,18) = \\textrm{gcd}(18,12) = \\textrm{gcd}(12,6) = 6\n$$\n\nIn this example, the algorithm concluded in three steps, but the argument can be extended to many steps." 13194139533570 17592186045417 false] [17592186153380 1064 "We now return to the question of why the Euclidean algorithm works. To help us to understand, let's consider once more how to compute $\\textrm{gcd}(48,18).$ Using the algorithm, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}48 & = & 18(2) & + & 12 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 18 & = & 12(1) & + & 6 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 12 & = & 6(2) & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCarrying out the process gives the following pairs of integers:\n\n$$\n48,18\\quad \\longrightarrow\\quad 18,12 \\quad \\longrightarrow \\quad 12,6\n$$\n\nWe claim that $\\textrm{gcd}(48,18)$ must be the same as $\\textrm{gcd}(18,12).$ To see why, notice that we used the division algorithm to write\n\n$$\n48  =  18(2)  +  12.\n$$\n\nIf $48$ and $18$ are divisible by some integer $n,$ then $n$ must also divide $18(2).$ This means that $n$ must divide $48-18(2),$ i.e., $n$ must divide $12.$ So any number $n$ that divides both $48$ and $18$ must also divide both $18$ and $12.$  \n\nRepeating the same argument, we are able to conclude that $\\textrm{gcd}(18,12) = \\textrm{gcd}(12,6).$ Since $\\textrm{gcd}(12,6) = 6,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(48,18) = \\textrm{gcd}(18,12) = \\textrm{gcd}(12,6) = 6.\n$$\n\nIn this example, the algorithm concluded in three steps, but the argument can be extended to many steps." 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409445 1080 "39" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409445 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409445 1083 #uuid "01592b2f-8e81-5ca0-bfa0-bfc6f3b61a9c" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409446 1080 "22" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409446 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409446 1083 #uuid "024a7e0b-4683-51db-a9ad-cca4f4ab8a3c" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409447 1080 "27" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409447 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409447 1083 #uuid "042cfcd9-4141-5040-af7a-11da39d2f295" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409448 1080 "43" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409448 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409448 1083 #uuid "16d39c9a-b022-5605-a137-5bd4b9d862dd" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409449 1080 "II and III only" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409449 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409449 1083 #uuid "22a76842-1f68-5a89-9f31-4d47c4cc64a9" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409450 1080 "I and III only" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409450 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409450 1083 #uuid "26eed0b8-bca8-534f-9777-f36e6dec7b63" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409451 1080 "36" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409451 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194