17592186409480 1077 "selection" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409480 1078 #edb/ref 17592186409472 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409480 1079 #uuid "bab5c137-e686-5330-940a-11463985a910" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409477 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409481 1069 "q-132699" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409481 1071 "Find the greatest common divisor of $490$ and $154.$" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409481 1072 #uuid "2c6a7c2f-ebd8-5018-a687-121a9ed1a14a" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409481 1074 "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}490 & = & 154(3) & + & 28 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 154 & = & 28(5) & + & 14 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 28 & = & 14(2) & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $14.$ Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $490$ and $154$ is $14.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(490,154)=14.\n$$" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409479 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409482 1069 "q-98480" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409482 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409482 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{gcd}(765,221)=17$\n- $\\text{gcd}(765,221)=1$\n- $765$ and $221$ are coprime" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409482 1072 #uuid "3936dcf8-3c18-5d25-b1ef-fee251049b9e" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409482 1074 "Recall that two integers are coprime if their greatest common divisor is $1.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Indeed, let's use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}765 & = & 221\\cdot 3 & + & 102 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 221 & = & 102\\cdot 2 & + & 17 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 102 & = & 17\\cdot 6 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $17.$ So, the greatest common divisor of $765$ and $221$ is $17:$\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(765,221)=17.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{gcd}(765,221)>1,$ the numbers are not coprime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409480 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409483 1069 "q-98476" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409483 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409483 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=1$\n- $\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=3$\n- $5719$ and $2669$ are coprime" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409483 1072 #uuid "9e2b9768-6303-5aa7-8a02-db48065ae94a" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409483 1074 "Recall that two integers are coprime if their greatest common divisor is $1.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Indeed, let's use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}5719 & = & 2669\\cdot 2 & + & 381 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 2669 & = & 381\\cdot 7 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 381 & = & 2\\cdot 190 & + & 1 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 2 & = & 1\\cdot 2 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $1.$ So, the greatest common divisor of $5719$ and $2669$ is $1:$\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=1,$ the numbers are coprime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409475 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409484 1069 "q-79654" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409484 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409484 1071 "Find the greatest common divisor of $2\\,522$ and $1\\,092.$" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409484 1072 #uuid "ee6022bc-35f0-5e0b-b95e-866627e7670f" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true] [17592186409484 1074 "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}2\\,522 & = & 1\\,092(2) & + & 338 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 1\\,092 & = & 338(3) & + & 78 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 338 & = & 78(4) & + & 26 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 78 & = & 26(3) & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $26.$ Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $2\\,522$ and $1\\,092$ is $26.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(2\\,522,1\\,092)=26.\n$$" 13194139533570 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-01592b2f-8e81-5ca0-bfa0-bfc6f3b61a9c" 17592186409445 "answer-024a7e0b-4683-51db-a9ad-cca4f4ab8a3c" 17592186409446 "answer-042cfcd9-4141-5040-af7a-11da39d2f295" 17592186409447 "answer-16d39c9a-b022-5605-a137-5bd4b9d862dd" 17592186409448 "answer-22a76842-1f68-5a89-9f31-4d47c4cc64a9" 17592186409449 "answer-26eed0b8-bca8-534f-9777-f36e6dec7b63" 17592186409450 "answer-2e32bdd0-f9d1-58b1-8b93-45c8d5b59754" 17592186409451 "answer-32bfcd7d-9a8e-5097-bc46-02425b35eb67" 17592186409452 "answer-37a34d4f-dba5-52ed-aee1-a93037faa536" 17592186409453 "answer-38281564-4727-50bc-a7f5-8a2abe6f2fe5" 17592186409454 "answer-396b6544-d108-5e60-a16f-f4d6ee7d2dd3" 17592186409455 "answer-3d952b6c-e25b-5f69-9d2a-ff0c63a4d7a9" 17592186409456 "answer-56cc2837-afdc-5789-99d5-e022a2cce2f6" 17592186409457 "answer-5f971696-c2bb-5172-9655-1e8f79d3ca37" 17592186409458 "answer-69a86ecf-fc9b-50ae-b2d4-ff14e18b2347" 17592186409459 "answer-7670efc5-9bf9-5b1e-bd6d-fa7dd6519896" 17592186409460 "answer-8a9fde34-a565-5dcd-91cd-3bb595bab139" 17592186409461 "answer-98240c83-8994-519e-a548-3061729c276a" 17592186409